\import{spin-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{characters, class functions, and the character table}
\tag{math}
\tag{fgap}
\tag{group-algebra}
\tag{representation}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.ClassFunction.basisOfIrreducibleCharacters,TauCeti.characterTable,TauCeti.card_inv_mul_sum_characterTable_mul_characterTable_inv,TauCeti.card_conjClass_mul_sum_characterTable_mul_characterTable_inv}

\p{For a finite-dimensional representation #{\rho} over a
characteristic-zero field, its character is
#{\chi_\rho(g)=\operatorname{tr}(\rho(g))}. Trace is unchanged by
conjugation, so #{\chi_\rho} is a class function. A \newvocab{character
table} places the irreducible characters in rows and the conjugacy classes in
columns. Tau Ceti identifies class functions with functions on conjugacy
classes in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.ClassFunction.equivConjClasses}.}

\p{Over #{\mathbb C}, the irreducible characters form an orthonormal basis of
the class functions. Thus the table is square: the number of irreducible
characters equals the number of conjugacy classes. This equality also
appears as the equality between the number of Wedderburn blocks and the
dimension of the center. See
\citet{thm. 16.4, pp. 159--166}{james2001representations}.}
